window.docContents[198].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-maximum-degree-at-least-three":{"contents":"From now on we assume that Δ ≥ 3. The classical greedy colouring proof due to\nLovász (1975) uses the blocks of a graph, its maximal connected subgraphs without a\ncut vertex, which we study in the next section,\nCut Vertices And Blocks. Instead, the proof we give is a variation\nof Lovász's approach that avoids blocks: it uses partial colourings and the idea\nbehind greedy colouring, but replaces the need for 2-connected blocks with strong\ninduction on vertex subsets and a gluing step across a pair of non-adjacent\nvertices.\n\nThe basic idea is to colour vertices greedily but in an order chosen so that we never\nreach an uncoloured vertex with Δ distinctly coloured neighbours, hence the greedy\ncolouring will never use more than Δ colours.\n\nSketch Proof: we prove the stronger statement that every finite subset of the\nvertices s : Finset V satisfies G.ColorableOn Δ ↑s, by strong induction on s\nunder ⊂, so our induction hypothesis is:\n\nih : ∀ t ⊂ s, G.ColorableOn Δ ↑t.\n\nDegeneracy reduction: if some a ∈ s has less than Δ neighbours in s, then\napply ih to s.erase a and extend the resulting colouring to a using a free\ncolour (the neighbours of a use less than Δ colours).\n\nSo wlog every vertex of s has at least Δ neighbours in s. Since all degrees are\nat most Δ, every vertex of s must have exactly Δ neighbours, all of which lie\nin s (so s is closed under adjacency).\n\nRegular case: write K := s, which is closed under adjacency and Δ-regular, and\npick any v₂ ∈ K. We do not need K to be connected: the construction below works\nuniformly for any adjacency-closed Δ-regular K, using only colourings of proper\nsubsets of K. (If K were disconnected we could instead colour the component of\nv₂ and the rest separately, but this case split is unnecessary.)\n\nSince G has no Δ + 1-clique, the neighbourhood of v₂ is not a clique, so v₂\nhas two neighbours v₁ and v₃ that are not adjacent to each other. Write\nW := K \\ {v₁, v₃} for the remaining vertices of K. Let A be the vertex set of\nthe connected component of v₂ in the subgraph induced by W (the vertices of W\nthat can be reached from v₂ by a walk inside W), and let B := W \\ A be the\nrest, so there are no edges between A and B. (If the pair {v₁, v₃} does not\nseparate K then B is empty, but we do not need to treat this case separately.)\n\nNow D := A ∪ {v₁, v₃} and E := B ∪ {v₁, v₃} together cover K and meet only in\nthe non-adjacent pair {v₁, v₃}; moreover A, B and E are proper subsets of\nK (the first misses v₁, the others v₂), so ih applies to them. The plan is to\ncolour D and E separately and then glue them together using our\nColoringOn.union construction.\n\n1. D has a colouring in which v₁ and v₃ have the same colour.Colour v₁ and v₃ first, both with colour 0 (legal, since they are not\n   adjacent). Then colour the vertices of A other than v₂ greedily, in\n   reverse search order from v₂. That is, start with S = {v₂} and repeatedly\n   add to S a vertex of A outside S that has a neighbour in S; we say such\n   a vertex has been settled. Since A is connected, every vertex of A is\n   eventually settled. Now colour the vertices in the reverse of the order in which\n   they were settled. Each vertex was settled because of an edge to an\n   already-settled neighbour, which is either v₂ or a vertex coloured later; so\n   when a vertex is coloured it still has an uncoloured neighbour, hence at most\n   Δ - 1 coloured neighbours, and a free colour exists.Finally colour v₂: among its Δ neighbours are v₁ and v₃, which received\n   the same colour, so its neighbours use at most Δ - 1 colours and a free colour\n   remains.2. Gluing. Since the only edges from D to E \\ D ⊆ B leave from {v₁, v₃}\n   (there are no A–B edges), two colourings of D and E can be glued together\n   as long as they agree on {v₁, v₃}. Moreover they can be made to agree by\n   permuting the colours of one of them, provided that they agree on whether v₁\n   and v₃ receive equal colours. So it suffices to find colourings of D and E\n   with the same pattern (equal or distinct) on {v₁, v₃}.By Step 1, D has a colouring with v₁ and v₃ equal. Also, for\n   v ∈ {v₁, v₃} the Δ neighbours of v split between A and B, with at least\n   one (namely v₂) in A.Case (a). If v₁ and v₃ have at most Δ - 1 neighbours in B in total\n   (as happens when B is empty), colour B by ih and extend to E giving\n   v₁, v₃ a common free colour; pair this with the equal-pattern colouring of D.Case (b). Otherwise one of v₁, v₃, say v₁, has at least 2 neighbours in\n   B and hence at most Δ - 2 in A. The other, v₃, has at most Δ - 1\n   neighbours in A: since v₂ ∈ A, v₁ has at most Δ - 1 neighbours in B, so\n   v₃ has at least one there. So a colouring of A by ih extends to D giving\n   v₁, v₃ distinct colours. So D has colourings of both patterns, and we pair\n   the ih-colouring of E with whichever one matches it.\n\nsection brooks\nvariable {Δ : ℕ}\n\n\nopen Finset\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Brooks' Theorem*","header":"10.6.2. Maximum Degree at Least Three","id":"/Connectivity/#section-maximum-degree-at-least-three"},"/Constructions/#section-sum-and-sigma-graphs":{"contents":"Given graphs G : SimpleGraph V and H : SimpleGraph W, their (disjoint) sum\nG ⊕g H is the graph formed by placing a copy of G and a copy of H side by\nside, with no edges between them. Its vertex type is the sum type V ⊕ W, whose\nelements are either Sum.inl v with v : V or Sum.inr w with w : W. Two\nvertices Sum.inl v₁ and Sum.inl v₂ are adjacent iff G.Adj v₁ v₂, two\nvertices Sum.inr w₁ and Sum.inr w₂ are adjacent iff H.Adj w₁ w₂, and no\nvertex Sum.inl v is adjacent to any vertex Sum.inr w. Mathlib defines G ⊕g H\nby pattern matching on these cases.\n\n#source SimpleGraph.sum\n#check sum_adj_inl\n#check sum_adj_inr\n\n\nMore generally, we can take the disjoint union of an indexed family of graphs, which\nwe call a sigma graph. This is not currently in Mathlib, so we define it here. If\nthe graph indexed by i : ι has vertex type V i, then the vertex type of the\ndisjoint union is the dependent pair (or Sigma) type Σ i, V i.\n\n#source Sigma\n\n\nA vertex of Σ i, V i is a pair ⟨i, u⟩ consisting of an index i : ι and an\nelement u : V i. The pair is called dependent because the type of its second\ncomponent depends on the value of its first. Lean prints Σ i, V i as\n(i : ι) × V i, so expect to see that form in goals and #check output.\n\nTwo vertices ⟨i, u⟩ and ⟨j, v⟩ should be adjacent iff i = j and u and v\nare adjacent in G i. Stating this takes some care: u : V i but v : V j, so\n(G i).Adj u v does not typecheck. A proof h : i = j does not fix this on its\nown, because Lean only identifies types that are equal by definition, and V i and\nV j are not. Instead we use h to transport v along the equation, giving\nh ▸ v : V i.\n\nWe mark the definition protected, so that even inside namespace SimpleGraph it\nmust be called SimpleGraph.sigma (or Σg). This stops the short name sigma from\nclashing with other functions of that name, such as Finset.sigma.\n\n/-- The disjoint union of an indexed family of graphs. Given an index type ι and,\nfor each i : ι, a graph G i on the vertex type V i, the disjoint union Σg G\nhas vertex type Σ i, V i, and two vertices are adjacent iff they lie in the same\ngraph G i and are adjacent there. -/\n@[simps!]\nprotected def sigma {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} (G : (i : ι) → SimpleGraph (V i)) :\n    SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) where\n  Adj := fun ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ => ∃ h : i = j, (G i).Adj u (h ▸ v)\n  symm := by\n    constructor\n    rintro ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ ⟨rfl, hadj⟩\n    exact ⟨rfl, hadj.symm⟩\n\n\nIn the proof of symm above, rintro ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ ⟨rfl, hadj⟩ introduces the two\nvertices and splits the adjacency proof into its two parts. The pattern rfl\nsubstitutes i = j everywhere, after which h ▸ v reduces to v.\n\nThe substitution is needed because the cast h ▸ v only computes (in the sense of\nDefinitional And Propositional Equalities) when both sides of h\nare definitionally equal, in which case it reduces to v. While i and j are\ndistinct variables the cast is stuck, just like 0 + n, and no amount of unfolding\ngets past it to v. We will meet stuck casts again when we decide adjacency in\nDecidability And Kernel Reduction and when we change the\nendpoints of walks in Copy.\n\nThe @[simps!] attribute generates sigma_adj, in which the ⋯ hides the proof\nof i = j used by ▸. We write Σg G for SimpleGraph.sigma G.\n\n#check SimpleGraph.sigma_adj\n\n\n@[inherit_doc] scoped prefix:60 \"Σg \" => SimpleGraph.sigma\n\n\n/-- The disjoint union of the complete graphs K[0], K[1], K[2], …. -/\nabbrev natDisjointComplete : SimpleGraph (Σ i, Fin i) :=\n  Σg (fun (i : ℕ) => K[i])\n\n\nTwo vertices ⟨i, a⟩ and ⟨j, b⟩ of this graph are adjacent iff they lie in the\nsame complete graph (i = j) and are distinct. We cannot write a ≠ b here, since\na : Fin i and b : Fin j have different types; this is the same problem as\nabove. Comparing the underlying natural numbers (a : ℕ) and (b : ℕ) avoids it.\n\nIn the proof, t₁ <;> t₂ runs the tactic t₂ on every goal produced by t₁: here\nconstructor splits the ↔ into two implications, and rintro ⟨rfl, h⟩ then\nintroduces the hypotheses in both, substituting as in the sigma proof above.\n\nexample {i j : ℕ} {a : Fin i} {b : Fin j} :\n    natDisjointComplete.Adj ⟨i, a⟩ ⟨j, b⟩ ↔ i = j ∧ (a : ℕ) ≠ b := by\n  constructor <;> rintro ⟨rfl, h⟩\n  · exact ⟨rfl, Fin.val_ne_of_ne h⟩\n  · exact ⟨rfl, fun hab => h (congrArg Fin.val hab)⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Operations on Graphs","header":"3.3.3. Sum and Sigma Graphs*","id":"/Constructions/#section-sum-and-sigma-graphs"}});